\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs36 \f3\fs36 At the Great Thanksgiving}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 In making Eucharist, the Celebrant uses one of the Eucharistic Prayers\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 from Rite One or Rite Two, or one of the following forms}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 \par
}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22  \par
}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22  \par
}{\plain \fs24 \b\f3 Form 1}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 \par
}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 Celebrant}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 \ul0 \tab }{\plain \fs24 \f3 The Lord be with you. \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 People}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22   \ul0 \tab \ul0 \tab }{\plain \fs24 \f3 And also with you. \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 Celebrant}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22   \ul0 \tab }{\plain \fs24 \f3 Lift up your hearts. \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 People}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22   \ul0 \tab \ul0 \tab }{\plain \fs24 \f3 We lift them to the Lord. \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 Celebrant}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22   \ul0 \tab }{\plain \fs24 \f3 Let us give thanks to the Lord our God. \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 People}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22   \ul0 \tab \ul0 \tab }{\plain \fs24 \f3 It is right to give him thanks and praise.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 The Celebrant gives thanks to God the Father for his work in creation\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 and his revelation of himself to his people;\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 Recalls before God, when appropriate, the particular occasion being\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 celebrated;\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 Incorporates or adapts the Proper Preface of the Day, if desired.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 If the Sanctus is to be included, it is introduced with these or\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 similar words\par
}\pard \fs22\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20  }{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 And so we join the saints and angels in proclaiming your glory, as we \softline
sing (say),\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 Celebrant and People}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 \par
}\pard \fs22\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 Holy, holy, holy Lord, God of power and might,\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3  heaven and earth are full of your glory.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3     Hosanna in the highest.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3     Hosanna in the highest.\par
}\column 
\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 The Celebrant now praises God for the salvation of the world through\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 Jesus Christ our Lord.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 The Prayer continues with these words}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 \par
}\pard \fs22\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 And so, Father, we bring you these gifts. Sanctify them by\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 your Holy Spirit to be for your people the Body and Blood\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 of Jesus Christ our Lord.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 At the following words concerning the bread, the Celebrant is to hold\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 it, or lay a hand upon it; and at the words concerning the cup, to\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 hold or place a hand upon the cup and any other vessel containing wine\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 to be consecrated.}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 \par
}\pard \fs22\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 On the night he was betrayed he took bread, said the\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 blessing, broke the bread, and gave it to his friends, and \par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 said, {\u8220\'93}Take, eat: This is my Body, which is given for you.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 Do this for the remembrance of me.{\u8221\'94}\par
}\pard \fs24\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 After supper, he took the cup of wine, gave thanks, and\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 said, {\u8220\'93}Drink this, all of you; This is my Blood of the new\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 Covenant, which is shed for you and for many for the\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 forgiveness of sins. Whenever you drink it, do this for the\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 remembrance of me.{\u8221\'94}\par
}\pard \fs24\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 Father, we now celebrate the memorial of your Son. By\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 means of this holy bread and cup, we show forth the sacrifice\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 of his death, and proclaim his resurrection, until he comes\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 again.\par
}\pard \fs24\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 Gather us by this Holy Communion into one body in your\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 Son Jesus Christ. Make us a living sacrifice of praise.\par
}\pard \fs24\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 By him, and with him, and in him, in the unity of the Holy\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 Spirit all honor and glory is yours, Almighty Father, now and\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 for ever. }{\plain \fs24 \i\f3 AMEN.}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}{\plain \fs24 \b\f3 \par
}\column 
\pard \fs22
{\plain \fs24 \b\f3 Form 2}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 \par
}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 Celebrant}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 \ul0 \tab }{\plain \fs24 \f3 The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ and the love\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \tab \tab of God and the fellowship of the Holy Spirit be\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \tab \tab with you all.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 People}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22    \ul0 \tab }{\plain \fs24 \f3 And also with you.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 Celebrant}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22   \ul0 \tab }{\plain \fs24 \f3 Lift up your hearts.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 People}{\plain \fs24 \f3   \ul0 \tab \ul0 \tab We lift them to the Lord.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 Celebrant}{\plain \fs24 \f3   \ul0 \tab Let us give thanks to the Lord our God.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 People}{\plain \fs24 \f3   \ul0 \tab \ul0 \tab It is right to give him thanks and praise.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 The Celebrant gives thanks to God the Father for his work in creation\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 and his revelation of himself to his people;\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 Recalls before God, when appropriate, the particular occasion being\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 celebrated;\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 Incorporates or adapts the Proper Preface of the Day, if desired.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 If the Sanctus is to be included, it is introduced with these or similar \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 words}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}\pard \fs24\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 And so we join the saints and angels in proclaiming your glory, as we \softline
sing (say),\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 Celebrant and People}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}\pard \fs24\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 Holy, holy, holy Lord, God of power and might\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 heaven and earth are full of your glory.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3     Hosanna in the highest.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3     Hosanna in the highest.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 The Celebrant now praises God for the salvation of the world through\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 Jesus Christ our Lord.}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}\column 
\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 At the following words concerning the bread, the Celebrant is to hold\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 it, or lay a hand upon it; and at the words concerning the cup, to\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 hold or place a hand upon the cup and any other vessel containing wine\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 to be consecrated.}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}\pard \fs24\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 On the night he was handed over to suffering and death, our\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 Lord Jesus Christ took bread; and when he had given thanks\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 to you, he broke it, and gave it to his disciples, and said,\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 {\u8220\'93}Take, eat: This is my Body, which is given for you. Do this\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 for the remembrance of me.{\u8221\'94}\par
}\pard \fs24\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 After supper he took the cup of wine; and when he had given\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 thanks, he gave it to them, and said, {\u8220\'93}Drink this, all of you:\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 This is my Blood of the new Covenant, which is shed for you\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 and for many for the forgiveness of sins. Whenever you drink\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 it, do this for the remembrance of me.{\u8221\'94}\par
}\pard \fs24\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 Recalling now his suffering and death, and celebrating his\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 resurrection and ascension, we await his coming in glory.\par
}\pard \fs24\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 Accept, O Lord, our sacrifice of praise, this memorial of our\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 redemption.\par
}\pard \fs24\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 Send your Holy Spirit upon these gifts. Let them be for us\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 the Body and Blood of your Son. And grant that we who eat\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 this bread and drink this cup may be filled with your life and\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 goodness.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 The Celebrant then prays that all may receive the benefits of Christ's\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 work, and the renewal of the Holy Spirit.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \i\f3\fs20 The Prayer concludes with these or similar words}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}\pard \fs24\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 All this we ask through your Son Jesus Christ. By him, and\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 with him, and in him, in the unity of the Holy Spirit all honor\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 and glory is yours, Almighty Father, now and for ever.  }{\plain \fs24 \i\f3 AMEN.}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}\column 
\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs36 \i\f3\fs36 Additional Directions}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}\pard \fs24\sl360\slmult1 
{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 The Holy Table is spread with a clean white cloth during the celebration.\par
}\pard \fs20\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20  \par
}\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 When the Great Litany is sung or said immediately before the Eucharist,\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 the Litany concludes with the Kyries, and the Eucharist begins with the\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 Salutation and the Collect of the Day. The Prayers of the People following \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 the Creed may be omitted.\par
}\pard \fs20\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20  \par
}\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 When a psalm is used, it may be concluded with Gloria Patri. In Rite\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 One services, the following form of the Gloria may be used:}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 \par
}\pard \fs22\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 Glory be to the Father, and to the Son, *\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3   and to the Holy Ghost:\par
}\pard \fs24\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs24 \f3  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 As it was in the beginning, is now, and ever shall be, *\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3   world without end. Amen}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 .\par
}\pard \fs22\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22  \par
}\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 The Kyrie eleison (or {\u8220\'93}Lord, have mercy{\u8221\'94}) may be sung or said in\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 threefold, sixfold, or ninefold form. The Trisagion, {\u8220\'93}Holy God,{\u8221\'94} \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 may be sung or said three times, or antiphonally.\par
}\pard \fs20\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20  \par
}\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 Gloria in excelsis, or the hymn used in place of it, is sung or said from Christmas \softline
Day through the Feast of the Epiphany; on Sundays from\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 Easter Day through the Day of Pentecost, on all the days of Easter Week,\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 and on Ascension Day; and at other times as desired; but it is not used\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 on the Sundays or ordinary weekdays of Advent or Lent.\par
}\pard \fs20\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20  \par
}\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 It is desirable that the Lessons be read from a lectern or pulpit, and that \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 the Gospel be read from the same lectern, or from the pulpit, or from the midst of \softline
the congregation. It is desirable that the Lessons}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22  }{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 and Gospel be read from a book \softline
or books of appropriate size and dignity.\par
}\pard \fs20\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20  \par
}\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 When a portion of the congregation is composed of persons whose native\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 tongue is other than English, a reader appointed by the celebrant may\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 read the Gospel in the language of the people, either in place of, or in\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 addition to, the Gospel in English.\par
}\pard \fs20\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20  \par
}\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 If there is no Communion, all that is appointed through the Prayers of\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 the People may be said. (If it is desired to include a Confession of Sin, the}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 \par
}\column 
\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 service begins with the Penitential Order.) A hymn or anthem may then\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 be sung, and the offerings of the people received. The service may then\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 conclude with the Lord's Prayer; and with either the Grace or a blessing, \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 or with the exchange of the Peace.\par
}\pard \fs20\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20  \par
}\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 In the absence of a priest, all that is described above, except for the\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 blessing, may be said by a deacon, or, if there is no deacon, by a lay reader.\par
}\pard \fs20\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20  \par
}\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 The greeting, {\u8220\'93}The peace of the Lord be always with you,{\u8221\'94} is addressed\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 to the entire assembly. In the exchange between individuals which may \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 follow, any appropriate words of greeting may be used. If preferred, the\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 exchange  of the Peace may take place at the time of the administration of \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 the Sacrament (before or after the sentence of Invitation).\par
}\pard \fs20\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20  \par
}\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 Necessary announcements may be made before the service, after the\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 Creed, before the Offertory, or at the end of the service, as convenient.\par
}\pard \fs20\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20  \par
}\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 It is the function of a deacon to make ready the Table for the celebration, \softline
preparing and placing upon it the bread and cup of wine. It is customary \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 to add a little water to the wine. The deacon may be assisted by other ministers.\par
}\pard \fs20\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20  \par
}\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 During the Great Thanksgiving, it is appropriate that there be only \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 one chalice on the Altar, and, if need be, a flagon of wine from which\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 additional chalices may be filled after the Breaking of the Bread.\par
}\pard \fs20\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20  \par
}\pard \fs20
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 The following anthem may be used at the Breaking of the Bread:\par
}\pard \fs22\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22  \par
}\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs24 \f3 Lamb of God, you take away the sins of the world:\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3    have mercy on us.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 Lamb of God, you take away the sins of the world:\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3    have mercy on us.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 Lamb of God, you take away the sins of the world:\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3    grant us peace.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3  }{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 While the people are coming forward to receive Communion, the \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 celebrant receives the Sacrament in both kinds. The bishops, priests, and deacons at \softline
the Holy Table then communicate, and after them the}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22  }{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 people.\par
}\pard \fs20\sl120\slmult1 
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20  \par
}\pard \fs22
{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 Opportunity is always to be given to every communicant to receive the}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 consecrated Bread and Wine separately. But the Sacrament may be}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 \par
}{\plain \fs22 \f3\fs22 \par
}
